title: v6.0.4 e2e smoke test caught regex bug — saved a v5.21.1-style cascade
summary: 'Pre-publish e2e cohort-C smoke test caught a real publish-blocker bug in the migration-notice cohort-C predicate. Regex `/^\s*~/m` missed the standard YAML-list aspect format (`      - ~aspect-name`) — only matched mapping-key form (`  ~aspect:`). This repo wasn''t affected (compliance IS rostered, predicate short-circuits before .purpose walk), but ANY project authored with the canonical YAML-list .purpose format and without compliance rostered would have silently lost the migration notice + paradigm shift nomination. Fix was one-line: `/^\s*-?\s*~/m` (optional dash). Regression test added (9 isCohortC tests now). 313 tests pass / 1 skip (was 312). All 8 TCs in the smoke test now verified clean.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#migration-notices'
  - '#cohort-c-predicate'
  - '#aspect-detection-regex'
files_modified:
  - packages/paradigm/src/core/migration-notices.ts
  - packages/paradigm/src/core/migration-notices.test.ts
errors_encountered:
  - description: isCohortC regex /^\s*~/m missed YAML-list aspect format (- ~name) which is the standard .purpose convention; would have silently broken migration notice for most cohort-C projects on publish
    resolution: Changed regex to /^\s*-?\s*~/m (optional dash) + added regression test for YAML-list form
    time_to_fix: 5min
learnings:
  - E2E smoke tests catch bugs that unit tests can miss when the unit-test fixtures don't mirror real-world data shapes. Always seed fixtures from the actual repo conventions, not idealized minimal examples.
  - When a regex is the gating predicate for a behavior change, ALWAYS test against multiple representative content shapes (mapping-key form AND list-item form for YAML structures).
  - Pre-publish e2e walks pay for themselves on the first save. v5.21.1-v5.21.4 was 3 patches because we shipped without this gate; v6.0.4 caught its cascade-prevention here.
